Abdul Waheed v. China Eastern Airlines

— Dispute over international air passenger transport contract

【Ruling Summary】

Under the applicable international conventions, where one airline issues the ticket and physically performs part of the carriage, and another airline physically performs another part of the carriage in an air passenger transport, the two airlines are not successive carriers in the sense of aviation law. When a passenger seeks to hold an actual carrier liable for the carriage it performed, the passenger may choose the defendant. If the sued airline applies to join the other airline as a party to the proceedings, the court may decide whether to grant the application based on factors such as the actual needs of the case, litigation costs, and the convenience of the passenger in enforcing his rights.

If the passenger pays the full ticket price, the airline must provide complete transport services; if the passenger purchases a discounted ticket, the airline may correspondingly cancel certain services. However, an airline's notation on a discounted ticket of "non-refundable, non-endorsable" only restricts the passenger from refunding or endorsing the ticket for reasons attributable to himself, and cannot deprive the passenger of the right, after paying the ticket price, to be transported to the destination on time. When a flight delay caused by force majeure prevents an airline from transporting a passenger who needs to connect to another flight to the destination on time, the airline has the obligation to clearly inform the passenger at the point of departure whether onward endorsement services will be provided after arrival at the destination, and if such services cannot be provided, how the passenger should handle travel procedures. Under Articles 19 and 20(1) of the Warsaw Convention as amended at The Hague in 1955, if the airline fails to fulfill this obligation or cannot prove that it has fulfilled this obligation, and thereby causes loss to the connecting passenger, it shall be liable for compensation.

Plaintiff: ABDUL WAHEED, male, 34 years old, Pakistani national, residing at Lane 1, Shuiqing Yi Cun, Minhang District, Shanghai.

Defendant: China Eastern Airlines Co., Ltd., domiciled at: International Airport Avenue, Pudong New Area, Shanghai.

Legal Representative: Li Fenghua, Chairman of the Board of Directors.

The plaintiff, ABDUL WAHEED (hereinafter referred to as Abdul), filed a lawsuit with the Pudong New Area People's Court of Shanghai against the defendant, China Eastern Airlines Co., Ltd. (hereinafter referred to as China Eastern Airlines), concerning a dispute over an international air passenger transport contract.

Plaintiff Abdul alleged: The plaintiff, together with his wife, daughter, and mother-in-law (who have filed separate lawsuits), originally planned to fly from Shanghai to Karachi on December 31, 2004. Since there was no direct flight, the defendant China Eastern Airlines arranged for the plaintiff and others to first take its flight from Shanghai to Hong Kong, and then take a Hong Kong Cathay Pacific Airways flight to Karachi. For this purpose, the plaintiff paid the full fare from Shanghai to Karachi, and the company issued a special invoice for international air passenger transport and a ticket to the plaintiff. On December 31, 2004, the plaintiff waited at Shanghai Pudong Airport to board China Eastern Airlines flight MU703. Under normal circumstances, the flight was scheduled to depart from Shanghai at 11:00 and arrive in Hong Kong at 13:30. However, the flight was delayed by more than three hours that day, arriving in Hong Kong only at 16:30. While waiting at Pudong Airport, the plaintiff knew that he would miss the connecting Cathay Pacific flight and repeatedly inquired about how to handle the situation. Staff of China Eastern Airlines gave the plaintiff four copies of a "Continuation of Journey Registration Form." The plaintiff's wife filled in the forms and returned them to the company. Before boarding, the plaintiff repeatedly asked what would happen after arriving in Hong Kong. The China Eastern Airlines staff told him that since he had filled in the "Continuation of Journey Registration Form," they would help solve the problem. After flight MU703 arrived in Hong Kong, the Cathay Pacific flight had already departed. At Hong Kong Airport, a service agent took the plaintiff and others to a flight delay handling counter and then left. Later, a China Eastern Airlines staff member arrived. The plaintiff again asked what to do and was told that there were two options: one was for the plaintiff and others to wait at the airport for three days and then take the next Cathay Pacific flight, with all expenses for the three days to be borne by themselves; the other was for the plaintiff to purchase tickets from another airline to Karachi at his own expense, costing approximately HKD 25,000. The plaintiff stated that he could not accept either option, and the staff member then ignored the plaintiff. The plaintiff's wife had to call China Eastern Airlines, but was told that the relevant staff had already finished work. With no other choice, the plaintiff's wife pushed over the airport railing, attracting the attention of Hong Kong Airport security and police. After negotiations with the security and police, and since the plaintiff was carrying an eight-month-old daughter, he was compelled to purchase tickets from Emirates Airlines from Hong Kong via Dubai to Karachi, paying HKD 4,721 for the tickets and HKD 759 for baggage, totaling HKD 5,480. Arrival in Karachi was delayed by 15 hours compared to the originally scheduled itinerary, and the baggage did not arrive on the same flight. The plaintiff believed that China Eastern Airlines had failed to notify him in advance of the flight delay and failed to transport him from Shanghai to Hong Kong on time, constituting a breach of contract. After the flight delay, China Eastern Airlines also violated its own promises by refusing to re-arrange the itinerary from Hong Kong to Karachi for the plaintiff and failing to inform the plaintiff that he could change or transfer to another flight, constituting gross negligence. The actions of China Eastern Airlines caused substantial losses to the plaintiff and should be subject to compensation. The plaintiff requested the court to order China Eastern Airlines to compensate the plaintiff for economic losses of RMB 5,990; to order China Eastern Airlines to publicly disclose its flight punctuality rate and passenger complaint rate in accordance with its commitments; and to bear the litigation costs of this case.

Plaintiff Abdul submitted the following evidence:

1. A special invoice for international air passenger transport with a face value of RMB 22,647 and an international air passenger transport ticket and boarding pass, to prove that Abdul paid the full fare for the ticket from Shanghai to Karachi, established an international air passenger transport contract relationship with the defendant China Eastern Airlines, and boarded China Eastern Airlines flight MU703 from Shanghai to Hong Kong on December 31, 2004;

2. A ticket with a face value of HKD 4,721, a baggage ticket with a face value of HKD 759, and a boarding pass, to prove that Abdul was forced to purchase tickets again in Hong Kong and flew Emirates Airlines via Dubai to Karachi, arriving 15 hours later than the originally scheduled itinerary;

3. A fax sent by Abdul when booking the ticket, containing specific requirements, to prove that China Eastern Airlines knew that Abdul was carrying an eight-month-old infant and that Abdul's itinerary would involve a transfer in Hong Kong;

4. A letter dated May 23, 2005, from the Quality Service Department of China Eastern Airlines regarding the handling result of Abdul's complaint, to prove that China Eastern Airlines admitted that flight MU703, which Abdul boarded, was delayed, thereby causing Abdul and his family to miss the Cathay Pacific flight to Karachi after arriving in Hong Kong, but refused to compensate;

5. An article titled " 'Passengers' Views on Civil Aviation in 2004' – User Evaluation Results Released" published in *China Civil Aviation* magazine, to prove that China Eastern Airlines formulated and published a "Passenger Service Commitment System," promising to publish the company's flight punctuality rate quarterly and to vigorously address flight delay issues, but China Eastern Airlines did not publish the delay of this flight, violating its own commitment;

6. An article titled "Consumer Protection Commission to 'Advise' CAAC Next Month" published in the *Labor Daily* on July 20, 2005, to prove that flight delay issues had become a hot topic of complaints.

Defendant China Eastern Airlines argued: The ticket held by the plaintiff Abdul was for international consecutive air transport; the entire itinerary was to be performed separately by the defendant and Cathay Pacific Airways, with the defendant responsible for the Shanghai–Hong Kong segment. Abdul had no contract for the entire transport with the defendant, so the defendant bears no liability for the entire transport. As long as the defendant transported Abdul to Hong Kong, its obligation under the contract was fulfilled. The delay of flight MU703 on December 31, 2004, was caused by weather conditions, which constituted force majeure, and the defendant bears no liability for this delay. On that day, Abdul could have chosen to refund the ticket or to board the flight. Abdul voluntarily chose to board the flight despite knowing that he would miss the Cathay Pacific flight, so he should bear the responsibility himself. To ascertain the facts of this case, the defendant applied to join Cathay Pacific Airways as a third party to this case.

Defendant China Eastern Airlines submitted the following evidence:

1. A briefing from the Pudong Airport Operations Center on the operation situation, to prove that from December 30 to 31, 2004, due to snow and ice, a large number of flights at Pudong Airport were delayed;

2. A weather report issued by the Meteorological Center of the East China Regional Administration of the Civil Aviation Administration of China, to prove the weather conditions at Pudong Airport from December 30 to December 31, 2004;

3. A certificate issued by the Service Department of the Airport Terminal Management Branch, to prove that China Eastern Airlines had made broadcast announcements regarding the delay of flight MU703 on December 31, 2004;

4. A settlement list of expenses for irregular flight services, to prove that China Eastern Airlines provided beverages and meals to the delayed passengers;

5. A blank "Flight Delay Information Registration Form," to prove the items on the form filled in by the plaintiff Abdul at that time;

6. Document No. 538 of Civil Aviation East China [2002], to prove that the ticket sales office indicated on Abdul's invoice was not under the jurisdiction of China Eastern Airlines.

The court presided over the examination of evidence during the trial. Defendant China Eastern Airlines argued: The special invoice stamp on the invoice submitted by the plaintiff Abdul was forged; the defendant had no ticket office as listed on the invoice; the ticket submitted by Abdul was issued by Hong Kong Cathay Pacific Airways, and the defendant was only responsible for the Shanghai–Hong Kong segment of transport under that ticket; although Abdul's evidence 2 proved the time of Abdul's arrival in Karachi, as far as the defendant was concerned, flight MU703 was only delayed by three hours in arriving in Hong Kong due to weather, which was unrelated to the time of Abdul's arrival in Karachi; although Abdul's evidence 3 was authentic, it could not prove that the entire segment of Abdul's itinerary was the responsibility of the defendant; evidence 5 was media commentary and could not prove that the defendant had published a "Passenger Service Commitment System," and moreover, this matter was irrelevant to Abdul's claim; evidence 6 was irrelevant to this case. Abdul argued: Evidence 1 submitted by China Eastern Airlines did not prove that the weather conditions on December 31 made takeoff impossible; although evidence 2 proved that Pudong Airport was closed for one hour on December 30, 2004, due to snow, but there was no snow on the 31st, so flights should have been able to take off all day; evidence 3 proved that the time of China Eastern Airlines' broadcast of flight delay information was 10:58, only two minutes earlier than the original departure time, and even if the company did broadcast the flight delay information, such notification was flawed; evidence 4 could not show that China Eastern Airlines had fulfilled its obligations to the delayed passengers, because the plaintiff was a Muslim and could not accept the non-halal food provided by China Eastern Airlines; evidence 5 was not the form that China Eastern Airlines asked the plaintiff to fill in at Pudong Airport; the plaintiff had filled in a "Continuation of Journey Registration Form" at that time; the authenticity of evidence 6 could not be confirmed.

During the trial, the defendant China Eastern Airlines stated that the form filled in by the plaintiff Abdul at Pudong Airport on December 31, 2004, could no longer be found.

After examination and verification of evidence, the Pudong New Area People's Court of Shanghai found the following:

On December 29, 2004, the plaintiff Abdul purchased a ticket issued by Hong Kong Cathay Pacific Airways. The itinerary specified on the ticket was: Depart Shanghai at 11:00 on December 31 on the defendant China Eastern Airlines flight MU703 to Hong Kong; depart Hong Kong at 16:00 the same day on a Hong Kong Cathay Pacific Airways flight to Karachi. The terms on the back of the ticket stated that the contract should be subject to the rules and limitations of liability specified in the Warsaw Convention. The ticket was a discounted ticket, with a notation of non-refundable and non-endorsable. At 15:00 on December 30, 2004, moderate snow began to fall in the Pudong Airport area, and the airport was forced to close for one hour from 22:00 to 23:00, resulting in the delay of 104 flights that day. The next day, due to the need to handle aircraft de-icing and make-up flight scheduling, 43 flights departing from Pudong Airport were canceled and 142 were delayed, with an on-time departure rate of only 24.1%. On that day, flight MU703 was also delayed by 3 hours and 22 minutes in departure due to weather conditions, causing Abdul and his party to miss the connecting Cathay Pacific flight to Karachi upon arrival at Hong Kong Airport.

While waiting at Pudong Airport, the plaintiff Abdul and his family had already realized that the delayed arrival of flight MU703 in Hong Kong would cause them to miss the connecting Cathay Pacific flight, so they repeatedly went to the service counter of the defendant China Eastern Airlines to inquire about what to do. China Eastern Airlines staff asked Abdul to fill in a "Continuation of Journey Registration Form" and stated that after filling it in, they would help resolve the issue. After Abdul and his family arrived in Hong Kong, China Eastern Airlines staff informed Abdul of two options: first, to wait at Hong Kong Airport for three days and then take the next Cathay Pacific flight, with all expenses for the three days to be borne by themselves; second, to purchase tickets from another airline to Karachi at their own expense, costing approximately HKD 25,000. Abdul immediately stated that he could not accept either option. Abdul's wife, Du Lin, who was carrying an infant, also could not accept China Eastern Airlines' options, and in anxiety and agitation, called China Eastern Airlines but was told that the relevant staff had already finished work. Eventually, after negotiations with Hong Kong Airport staff, Abdul and his party purchased tickets and baggage tickets from Emirates Airlines and took that airline's flight via Dubai to Karachi. For this purpose, Abdul incurred ticket costs of HKD 4,721 and baggage costs of HKD 759, totaling HKD 5,480. During the trial, both parties agreed that the exchange rate between HKD and RMB was 1:1.07.

The Pudong New Area People's Court of Shanghai is of the opinion:

The plaintiff Abdul is a national of Pakistan. The ticket he purchased had a place of departure of Shanghai, China, and a destination of Karachi, Pakistan. Article 142, Paragraph 1 of the General Principles of the Civil Law of the People's Republic of China provides: "The application of law in civil relations with foreign elements shall be determined in accordance with the provisions of this Chapter." Paragraph 2 provides: "If any international treaty concluded or acceded to by the People's Republic of China contains provisions differing from those in the civil laws of the People's Republic of China, the provisions of the international treaty shall apply, unless the provisions are ones on which the People's Republic of China has declared reservations." Both China and Pakistan are parties to the "Convention for the Unification of Certain Rules Relating to International Carriage by Air, signed at Warsaw on 12 October 1929, as amended by the Protocol done at The Hague on 28 September 1955" (hereinafter referred to as "the Warsaw Convention as amended at The Hague in 1955") and the "Convention, Supplementary to the Warsaw Convention, for the Unification of Certain Rules Relating to International Carriage by Air Performed by a Person Other than the Contracting Carrier, signed at Guadalajara on 18 September 1961" (hereinafter referred to as "the Guadalajara Convention"). Therefore, these two international conventions are applicable to this case. Article 28(1) of the Warsaw Convention as amended at The Hague in 1955 provides: "An action for damages must be brought, at the option of the plaintiff, in the territory of one of the High Contracting Parties, either before the court of the domicile of the carrier or of his principal place of business, or before the court of the place where the carrier has an establishment by which the contract has been made, or before the court of destination." Article 32 provides: "Any clause contained in the contract and all special agreements entered into before the damage occurs by which the parties purport to infringe the rules laid down by this Convention, whether by deciding the law to be applied, or by altering the rules as to jurisdiction, shall be null and void." Accordingly, in the case where Abdul has brought suit based on the ticket, the Pudong New Area People's Court of Shanghai, People's Republic of China, has jurisdiction over this dispute concerning an international air passenger transport contract.

Article 1, Paragraph 2 of the Guadalajara Convention provides: "'Contracting carrier' means a person who as a principal makes a contract of carriage governed by the Warsaw Convention with a passenger or consignor or with a person acting on behalf of the passenger or consignor." Paragraph 3 provides: "'Actual carrier' means a person other than the contracting carrier, who, by virtue of authority from the contracting carrier, performs the whole or part of the carriage referred to in paragraph 2, but who is not with respect to such part a successive carrier within the meaning of the Warsaw Convention. Such authority is presumed in the absence of proof to the contrary." Article 7 provides: "An action for damages in respect of carriage performed by the actual carrier may be brought, at the option of the plaintiff, against that carrier or the contracting carrier, or against both together or separately. If the action is brought against only one of those carriers, that carrier shall have the right to require the other carrier to be joined in the proceedings, the procedure and effects of which shall be governed by the law of the court seized of the case." The ticket held by the plaintiff Abdul was issued by Hong Kong Cathay Pacific Airways, so the international air passenger transport contract relationship was established between Abdul and Hong Kong Cathay Pacific Airways. Hong Kong Cathay Pacific Airways is the contracting carrier. The defendant China Eastern Airlines had no direct international air passenger transport contract with Abdul and is not a successive carrier; it is only presumed, based on authorization from Hong Kong Cathay Pacific Airways, to be the actual carrier that performed the transport from Shanghai to Hong Kong as specified on the ticket. Abdul is entitled to choose to bring an action for liability against Hong Kong Cathay Pacific Airways or China Eastern Airlines, or both simultaneously. In this action for liability brought by Abdul solely against China Eastern Airlines, although China Eastern Airlines has the right to request Hong Kong Cathay Pacific Airways to join the proceedings, since the liability for flight delay that Abdul is pursuing arose during the Shanghai–Hong Kong segment performed by China Eastern Airlines, which is unrelated to Hong Kong Cathay Pacific Airways, and considering the facts of this case and the balance of litigation costs, there is no need to add Hong Kong Cathay Pacific Airways as a party to these proceedings.

《1955年在海牙修改的华沙公约》第十九条规定：“承运人对旅客、行李或货物在航空运输过程中因延误而造成的损失应负责任。”第二十条（1）款规定：“承运人如果证明自己和他的代理人为了避免损失的发生，已经采取一切必要的措施，或不可能采取这种措施时，就不负责任。”2004年12月31日的MU703航班由于天气原因发生延误，对这种不可抗力造成的延误，被告东方航空公司不可能采取措施来避免发生，故其对延误本身无需承担责任，但还需证明其已经采取了一切必要的措施来避免延误给旅客造成的损失发生，否则即应对旅客因延误而遭受的损失承担责任。事实是，在浦东机场时，原告阿卜杜勒由于预见到MU703航班的延误会使其错过香港国泰航空公司的衔接航班，曾多次向东方航空公司工作人员询问怎么办。东方航空公司应当知道国泰航空公司从香港飞往卡拉奇的衔接航班3天才有一次，更明知阿卜杜勒一行携带着婴儿，不便在中转机场长时间等候，有义务向阿卜杜勒一行提醒中转时可能发生的不利情形，劝告阿卜杜勒一行改日乘机。但东方航空公司没有这样做，却让阿卜杜勒填写《续航情况登记表》，并告知会帮助解决，使阿卜杜勒对该公司产生合理信赖，从而放心登机飞赴香港。鉴于阿卜杜勒一行是得到东方航空公司的帮助承诺后来到香港，根据当时具体情况，尽管阿卜杜勒一行所持机票上标注着不得退票、不得转签，东方航空公司也应当把阿卜杜勒一行签转给其他航空公司，以帮助其尽快飞抵卡拉奇。但是东方航空公司不考虑阿卜杜勒一行携带婴儿要尽快飞往卡拉奇的合理需要，向阿卜杜勒告知了要么等待3天乘坐下一航班且3天中相关费用自理，要么自费购买其他航空公司机票的所谓“帮助解决”方案，将阿卜杜勒一行陷入走无法走、留不能留的两难境地。东方航空公司没有采取一切必要的措施来避免因航班延误给旅客造成的损失发生，不应免责。阿卜杜勒是迫于无奈才自费购买其他航空公司的机票，对阿卜杜勒购票支出的5480港元损失，东方航空公司应承担赔偿责任。

原告阿卜杜勒要求被告东方航空公司按照承诺对外公布该公司的航班正常率和旅客投诉率，该诉讼请求与阿卜杜勒的私权无直接关联，故不予支持。

据此，上海市浦东新区人民法院于2005年12月21日判决：

一、被告东方航空公司应在本判决生效之日起10日内赔偿原告阿卜杜勒损失人民币586360元；

二、原告阿卜杜勒的其他诉讼请求不予支持。

案件受理费人民币249元，由原告阿卜杜勒负担人民币5元，被告东方航空公司负担人民币244元。

一审宣判后，东方航空公司不服，向上海市第一中级人民法院提起上诉称：作为承运人，在航班延误的情况下，只有将延误情况如实告知旅客的义务，并无要求旅客改变航程的权利。上诉人已经将由于天气原因造成航班延误的情况通知被上诉人阿卜杜勒，没有权利取消被上诉人飞往香港的订座。被上诉人的机票上，标注着“不得退票，不得转签”，无需上诉人另外提醒和告知。上诉人从未对被上诉人作出过任何承诺以使其产生合理信赖，而被上诉人也知道其必然错过香港国泰航空公司的衔接航班。旅客有选择航程的权利，被上诉人在其知情权未受任何侵犯的情况下自愿选择飞赴香港，上诉人对被上诉人因此受到的损失不应承担赔偿责任。此外，原审既承认上诉人有权申请另一承运人国泰航空公司参加诉讼，又对上诉人的申请不予支持，严重侵犯上诉人的诉讼权利。请求撤销原判，改判驳回被上诉人的诉讼请求。

被上诉人阿卜杜勒辩称：对到香港后会错过国泰航空公司的衔接航班，上诉人与被上诉人在浦东机场起飞前就已经知道，因此被上诉人才反复向上诉人的工作人员询问怎么办。上诉人让被上诉人填写《续航情况登记表》，并承诺帮助解决。被上诉人的机票上标注“不得退票，不得转签”，不仅被上诉人知道，上诉人也知道。在这种情况下上诉人的工作人员承诺帮助解决，当然意味着帮助转签其他飞往卡拉奇的航班，而被上诉人也知道香港机场有这样的航班。在此情况下，被上诉人及家人才放心登上MU703航班飞往香港。被上诉人到达香港机场后，上诉人才提出他们的“帮助”方案，竟然是让被上诉人自费在香港机场等3天，或者自费购买其他公司的机票。这样的方案，就是没有上诉人帮助，被上诉人难道还想不出来？没有上诉人的“帮助”，被上诉人就会在上海自己的家中等3天，生活舒适花费又少，强过上诉人的“帮助”方案。上诉人收取了从上海到卡拉奇的全程费用，却在自己的航班延误后拒绝给被上诉人重新安排航程，最终导致被上诉人遭受精神折磨和经济损失，故理应承担赔偿责任。原判认定事实清楚，判处正确，应当维持。

上海市第一中级人民法院经审理，确认一审认定的事实无误。

二审应解决的焦点问题是：1.在以实际承运人为被告的责任诉讼中，实际承运人申请追加订约承运人参加诉讼，法院能否不予允许？2.在航班由于天气原因延误的情况下，航空公司对旅客应承担何种责任？3.对持有注明“不得退票，不得转签”机票的换乘旅客，航空公司可否一律不予转签？

上海市第一中级人民法院认为：

根据《瓜达拉哈拉公约》第七条的规定，在被上诉人阿卜杜勒以作为实际承运人的上诉人东方航空公司为被告提起的责任诉讼中，东方航空公司虽然有权申请国泰航空公司参加诉讼，但这种申请能否被允许，应由受理案件的法院决定。一审认为香港国泰航空公司与阿卜杜勒要追究的航班延误责任无关，故根据本案案情，衡量诉讼成本，决定不追加香港国泰航空公司为本案的当事人，并无不当。

无论何种原因发生航班延误后，被滞留的旅客都有权在第一时间获取尽可能详细的信息，并及时了解后续进展情况，以便根据延误情形对自己的旅途作出最合理选择；航空公司有义务及时播报航班延误信息，并有义务根据每一位滞留旅客的不同需要，向其提供航空公司掌握的其他旅途信息，以便该旅客作出正确抉择。MU703航班由于天气原因延误后，作为旅途被阻滞的旅客，被上诉人阿卜杜勒必然十分关心自己的旅途。其已意识到乘坐延误的MU703航班到达香港后，会错过国泰航空公司的衔接航班，于是多次向上诉人东方航空公司询问如何处理。东方航空公司让阿卜杜勒填写了《续航情况登记表》，并表示填好表格后会帮助解决。东方航空公司承认让阿卜杜勒填写过表格，但认为阿卜杜勒填写的是《航班延误信息登记表》，不是《续航情况登记表》。无论是《续航情况登记表》亦或是《航班延误信息登记表》，对阿卜杜勒的妻子杜琳在登机前填写的表格，东方航空公司始终未能提供，无法证明阿卜杜勒一行是在明知会对自己不利的情形下仍选择登机。通过登机前的申报登记和填表，东方航空公司应当知道阿卜杜勒一行是去香港转乘国泰航空公司飞往卡拉奇的航班，也应当知道这个航班3天才开行一次，更知道阿卜杜勒如果乘国泰航空公司下一航班，就要在中转机场滞留3天且费用自理。在此情况下，东方航空公司有义务将这些不利情况告知阿卜杜勒,以便其自行选择是否乘坐延误的MU703航班飞往香港。东方航空公司不尽这些义务，反而让阿卜杜勒一行填写《续航情况登记表》，并承诺帮助解决，故一审认定东方航空公司“没有采取一切必要的措施来避免因航班延误给旅客造成的损失”，是正确的。

上诉人东方航空公司主张，在MU703航班延迟到达香港后，因为被上诉人阿卜杜勒所持的是注明“不得退票，不得转签”的打折机票，其才拒绝给阿卜杜勒一行转签其他航空公司的飞机；而阿卜杜勒对自己的机票“不得退票，不得转签”也是清楚的，无须其另行提醒和告知。机票是国际航空旅客运输合同存在的凭证。旅客支付了足额票款，航空公司就要为旅客提供完整的运输服务。旅客购买了打折机票，航空公司当然也可以相应地取消一些服务。航空公司在打折机票上注明“不得退票，不得转签”，只是限制支付了打折票款的旅客由于自身原因而退票和转签，但不能剥夺旅客在支付了票款后享有的按时乘坐航班抵达目的地的权利。当MU703航班因不可抗力延迟起飞时，东方航空公司和阿卜杜勒都知道该航班抵达香港后，肯定会错过国泰航空公司的衔接航班；如果要飞往卡拉奇，则必需转签机票。东方航空公司既然不准备在香港机场给注明“不得退票，不得转签”的机票办理转签手续，就有义务在始发机场向阿卜杜勒明确告知，劝阻其乘坐延误的MU703航班。东方航空公司不尽此项义务，以至阿卜杜勒在相信该公司会转签机票的情况下乘坐MU703航班抵达香港，由此陷入既无法走又不能留的艰难处境，无奈之下只得另行购票。东方航空公司不负责任的处理方式，显然是造成阿卜杜勒机票损失的根本原因。东方航空公司片面强调MU703航班是由于不可抗力造成延误，该公司已将航班延误信息通知给阿卜杜勒，并遵从阿卜杜勒的意愿将其运抵香港，完成了国际航空旅客运输合同中己方的义务，主张阿卜杜勒的剩余航程与己无关。事实是，如果东方航空公司事先能将飞往香港后的种种不利后果明确告知阿卜杜勒，则阿卜杜勒的损失就有可能避免，故一审认定东方航空公司在始发地未尽告知和提醒义务，根据《1955年在海牙修改的华沙公约》第十九条、第二十条（1）款的规定判令其承担赔偿责任，并无不当。

综上所述，上诉人东方航空公司未能以证据证明乘机赴香港是被上诉人阿卜杜勒在未受其任何影响的情况下自愿作出的选择，也未能以证据证明其为了避免因航班延误给旅客造成损失而采取了一切必要的措施，故其提出的上诉理由不能成立，上诉请求不予支持。据此，依照《中华人民共和国民事诉讼法》第一百五十三条第一款第（一）项规定，上海市第一中级人民法院于2006年2月24日判决：

驳回上诉，维持原判。

上诉案件受理费人民币249元，由上诉人东方航空公司负担。

本判决为终审判决。

